GCC (GNU Compiler Collection) is a collection of
compilers used to compile programs written in
languages such as C, C++ and others.

For C programming, the GCC compiler converts the
C source code into machine code and produces
an executable file.

The basic command to compile a C program is:

gcc program.c

It creates an executable file named a.out by
default. The program can be executed using:

./a.out

Example:
gcc hello.c
./a.out
